#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125550} :activity/id #uuid "4c07cab7-a385-5dcf-8fac-281a4be4946f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049421 :topic/id #uuid "f3745544-3239-55ba-94eb-3deb858524c5" :topic/math-academy-id 1941} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125550 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125844 :tutorial/content "We can extend the mean value theorem from single-variable calculus to functions of multiple variables.\n\nFirst, let's recall the mean value theorem from single-variable calculus.\n\n*If $f(x)$ is a function that's continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, then there exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that* $$f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}.$$\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the above equation as follows:\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) = f'(c) (b-a)$$\n\nNow, we're ready to present the **multivariable mean value theorem,** which states the following:\n\n*If $f(x,y)$ is a function that's differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf{c}$ such that $$f(\\mathbf{b}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{c}) \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}),$$ where $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively.*\n\nThe theorem naturally extends to functions of three variables.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the function $$f(x,y)= x^2 + y^2$$ and the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 1,-1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle 2,2 \\rangle.$ A sketch of this situation is given below.\n\n![](images/67/67c44030beb47bf9cc53a3550da39d3a467fe45445c722803a3a8eaf70f1cb8a.png)\n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ we can apply the mean value theorem. That is, there exists a point $C\\in\\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf{c}$ such that\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ f(2,2)-f(1,-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,2⟩-⟨1,-1⟩) \\\\ ({2}^{2}+{2}^{2})-({1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ 6 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWriting out the components of $\\nabla f (\\mathbf c)$ and expanding the dot product, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩ & =6 \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩ & =6 \\\\ {f}_{x}(c)+3{f}_{y}(c) & =6.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the above equation *must* be satisfied by some point $\\mathbf c$ that lies on $\\overline{AB}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "92fbd1f5-a176-558c-9d91-081244c080f7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5095 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "75d67410-614f-5676-9156-2ea05fc7c4ec" :step/math-academy-id 31980 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125557}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125551 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124606 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124418 :question/id #uuid "a7899e04-8be9-597a-a9b6-111201ebad2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8607"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebec626a-be3d-5920-9588-48f3df108944" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388181}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Two-Variable Functions"} :step/id #uuid "817eece4-7f2b-59e4-8c4a-27a84ac89bb2" :step/math-academy-id 31981 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125552}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125552 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124607 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124419 :question/id #uuid "2b224972-6a43-5691-9e46-abff577311d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8608"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f9ea905-6eb5-55fe-b487-b115406bbc46" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048850}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399517}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Three-Variable Functions"} :step/id #uuid "1c6fc534-5695-5be4-8fd3-cc2b4b04687d" :step/math-academy-id 31982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124611 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124423 :question/id #uuid "0368b61d-2d85-58af-9af0-ec09edf877b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8693"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45abb029-42f5-5297-b793-f893178e0c29" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050143}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363045}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation Implied by the Mean Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "00db5d1b-5ee1-52e1-8187-7aa61cad3614" :step/math-academy-id 32303 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125551}}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Mean-Value Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124733}]}
